// @check
// @error An i32-keyed Map isn't supported yet
// @error An i32-keyed Map isn't supported yet
// DIAGNOSTIC (D-MAPSPELL): the actionable i32-key message fires for a map spelling that is
// NOT the whole annotation — a LIST of i32-keyed maps and a NULLABLE one. Both open `{[`
// and neither ends `}`, which is the exact width the MAP-NAME grammar's KEY CUT
// (`mapSpellKeyName`) is deliberately NOT gated on: the key ends at its own `]`, so the cut
// answers `"i32"` for `{[i32]:i32}[]` and `{[i32]:i32}|null` just as it does for the bare
// `{[i32]:i32}` that `error-i32-keyed.vl` already pins.
//
// This is the fixture that keeps the width honest. The VALUE cut (`mapSpellValName`) DOES
// take the closing `}`, because a value has no right edge without it; the key does not, and
// a slice that homed both behind one span gate would silently downgrade these two lines to
// a bare `unknown type '{[i32]:i32}[]'`. Measured before it was written: a divergence probe
// over the whole corpus found ZERO files in which the key cut answers for a name that is not
// a whole map span, so the population this width protects had no witness at all.
let xs: {[i32]: i32}[] = []
let m: {[i32]: i32} | null = null
